<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumbaya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first collected <a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kum ba yah, Come by Here</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Kum ba yah&quot;, &quot;Come by here&quot;, is an African-American spiritual whose origins are disputed. It is known to have been sung in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gullah">Gullah</a> culture of the islands off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, which has ties to enslaved Central Africans; Gullah is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> language of the formerly enslaved Africans and their descendants on the Sea Islands and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Stephen Winick of the Library of Congress, the song almost certainly arose among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gullah">Gullah</a> version appearing early even if it did not begin in that dialect. The two oldest versions of certain year were both collected in 1926 and are kept in the American Folklife Center: a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of words without music, gathered in Alliance, North Carolina, by the student Minnie Lee for her teacher Julian P. Boyd, and a wax cylinder recorded by the folklorist Robert Winslow Gordon of a singer named H. Wylie, within a few hours' drive of Darien, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><p>Members of the Society for the Preservation of Spirituals collected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gullah">Gullah</a> version, Come by Yuh, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> coast at some time between 1922 and 1931, and in May 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lomax">John Lomax</a> found Ethel Best singing Come by Here with a group in Raiford, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. These records contradict the long-standing attribution to the Reverend Marvin V. Frey, who claimed to have written the song around 1936 and said the title Kum Ba Yah came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> in 1946. Winick points out that in Wylie's dialect here is pronounced yah, which gives the phrase directly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Cooperative Recreation Service published the song in its Song Sampler pamphlet of January 1956, and it became a standard campfire song in Scouting and summer camps. The Folksmiths, among them Joe Hickerson, recorded it in 1957 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> in 1958, and Little Sugar and the Hightower Brothers released a gospel version on the Savoy label that year. Joan Baez's recording of 1962 spread it widely during the folk revival, and it became associated with the civil rights movement: marchers were recorded singing it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches">Selma to Montgomery march</a> of 1965.</p><p>From the 1990s the title entered the idiom of American politics, often used sarcastically to suggest that someone is too eager to compromise; Richard Vatz described such references as criticism of a consensus that does not examine the issues. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barack-obama">Barack Obama</a>, Mike Huckabee and Herman Cain were among the politicians who used the phrase.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kumbaya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
